  <section id="takeaways" class="case-study-section">
    <h2>Key Takeaways</h2>
    
    <div class="takeaways-grid">
      <div class="takeaway">
        <h3>🎯 Business Value First</h3>
        <p>
          ShopStream didn't adopt CDC for technical elegance—they did it to solve real business 
          problems. The Black Friday incident gave them a clear ROI story to tell leadership.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="takeaway">
        <h3>🔧 Right Tool for the Job</h3>
        <p>
          They chose Debezium despite higher complexity because it aligned with their strategic 
          direction. For teams without Kafka skills or different constraints, Fivetran or DMS 
          might be the better choice.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="takeaway">
        <h3>📈 Incremental Migration</h3>
        <p>
          Table-by-table migration reduced risk and allowed learning from each phase. Running 
          dual systems briefly was worth the redundancy for peace of mind.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="takeaway">
        <h3>👀 Observability Is Non-Negotiable</h3>
        <p>
          End-to-end monitoring caught issues that component metrics missed. The canary record 
          pattern and freshness checks were game-changers.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="takeaway">
        <h3>📚 Documentation & Training</h3>
        <p>
          Runbooks, schema evolution guides, and failure drills made the team resilient. 
          Investing in knowledge sharing paid dividends when incidents occurred.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="takeaway">
        <h3>🚀 CDC as a Platform</h3>
        <p>
          The real win wasn't just replacing ETL—it was building an event streaming platform 
          that enabled 18 new use cases in 6 months. Think of CDC as infrastructure, not a 
          point solution.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

